# Cursor Cloud specific instructions

These notes apply only to Cursor Cloud agents (kept out of the shared `AGENTS.md` since T3 Code is
developed in many places). They cover this environment's toolchain and non-obvious run caveats.

## Toolchain

Login shells (`bash -lc`) source `~/.profile`. That loads Vite+ and the Rust prefix.

- `vp` manages Node **26.12.1** (`package.json` `engines.node`) and pnpm **12.14.0**. `vp`, `node`, and `pnpm` resolve from `~/.local/share/vite-plus/bin`.
- Non-login shells see `/exec-daemon/node` (Node 22) first. Run repo commands in a login shell (`bash -lc '...'`).
- Rust stable is required for `native/resource-monitor` (edition 2024). `~/.profile` sets `RUSTUP_HOME=/usr/local/rustup` and `CARGO_HOME=/usr/local/cargo`. The agent wrapper sets those too, but a clean login shell does not inherit them.
- Install and refresh dependencies with `vp i` (the environment install uses `vp i --frozen-lockfile`). Do not run repo-wide `vp check`, `vp run -r test`, or `vp run -r typecheck` unless asked. `vp run check:changed` is the branch check.

## Running the web app

`vp run dev` from the repo root starts the server and the Vite web app. Read ports from the `[dev-runner]` line. The usual pair is web **5733** and server **13773**. Vite listens on `localhost` (`::1`). `http://127.0.0.1:5733` is connection refused. `http://localhost:5733` works.

On boot, `start` launches that dev server in tmux session `t3-dev` and waits until the log contains `pairingUrl:`. Logs are `/tmp/t3-dev.log`. If `t3-dev` already exists, do not start a second server.

Open the full `pairingUrl` from that log before the UI can talk to the server. Do not set `VITE_HTTP_URL` or `VITE_WS_URL`. A non-worktree checkout stores state under `~/.t3` (see the `baseDir=` line). A git worktree uses that worktree's `.t3`. Never point a server at `~/.t3/userdata`.

`vp run dev` registers the free `Replay (dev)` provider. External provider CLIs are not installed, so the UI loads and you can add a project, but a paid agent turn needs that provider's CLI.

## Mobile

This Linux image does not include the Android SDK or an emulator, and iOS builds need macOS. Use the web app here. Native mobile builds stay on a machine with the mobile toolchain.

## Known flaky tests (unrelated to setup)

- `apps/server/src/git/GitManager.test.ts` (cross-repo PR metadata, can time out at 12s).
- `apps/server/src/provider/ProviderRegistry.test.ts` (codex binaryPath re-probe ordering).
